  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return today through \nSaturday with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nAn upper trough is located over the Great Lakes early this \nmorning, with a ridge over the subtropical western Atlantic, \nwhich is resulting in SW flow aloft over the Mid-Atlantic. At \nthe surface, high pressure has settled offshore. Partly cloudy \nto mostly clear early this morning with temperatures ranging \nfrom the mid 60s to lower 70s, with some mid 70s along the \nimmediate coast. There is a subtle wave in the mid-level flow \nthat is triggering a few showers over eastern NC. Some of this \nactivity may nudge into NE NC from the northern shore of the \nAlbemarle Sound to the northern Outer Banks through the morning \nhours. 00z/27 CAMs/HREF remain rather unimpressive with respect \nto convection coverage and heavy rain threat for today, showing \nisolated to minimally scattered showers. PoPs remain low, mainly\nslight chc, with some low chc across NE NC/far SE VA and the NW\nPiedmont counties, which are closer to a boundary in vicinity \nof the Blue Ridge. The severe threat seems low for today with \nonly 20-25kt of 500mb flow and relatively weak mid- level lapse \nrates. However, a few isolated stronger tstms are possible with \nenough surface heating, mainly NW.\n\nThe boundary drops SE tonight and settles into interior SE VA \nand the Carolina Coastal Plain by Friday, but remains rather \ndiffuse. There is a strengthening jet core over New England, but\nthere remains some uncertainty as to how well the RRQ of this \nfeature interacts with the surface boundary. Friday is now \nwithin range of the 00z/27 HREF, which does have a decent signal\nfor locally heavy rain from showers/tstms with a contour of a \n30% chc of 3\"/3hr across eastern/SE VA and NE NC. The main \nthreat will be locally heavy rainfall and a Day 2 Slight ERO \ncovers NE NC and SE VA. However, showers/tstms will be more \nscattered in nature and this does cause some lower confidence \nwith respect to flooding. PoPs are highest (50- 70%)from the \nI-85 corridor northward to the RIC metro and east to lower \nEastern Shore and SE VA/NE NC, with 25-50% elsewhere. A few \nstronger tstms are possible as well with SW flow of 20-25kt \npersisting at 500mb, with the primary threat being damaging wind\ngusts. The front lingers in vicinity of the NC/VA state line \nSaturday, which could bring additional showers/tstms to the SE \nVA/NE NC area, especially in the afternoon (40-60% PoPs). \nLocally heavy rainfall will be possible in any showers/tstms. \nDrier conditions arrive by Sunday with only low PoPs over \ninterior SE VA/interior NE NC.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid today through Saturday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s, and nudging up \nto the upper 80s/around 90F (mid 80s at the coast) Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to \nmiddle portion of next week.\n\nHot conditions return early-mid next week as a 1.5-2.0 st dev \n500mb ridge builds from the Central Plains to the Southeast. NBM\nhigh temperatures Monday through Wednesday are primarily in the\nlower to mid 90s. However, 850mb temperatures are primarily \n19-21C in the 00z/27 EPS/GEFS, with similar values in the \ndeterministic 00z ECMWF. This along with 1000-850mb thickness \nschemes support highs in the mid to upper 90s. The 00z GFS is \neven hotter, but is an outlier at this time. If the hotter \ntemperatures are realized that will mean deeper mixing, which \nshould allow dewpoints to drop into the upper 60s/around 70F \nduring peak heating. Regardless, this would support heat indices\ninto the lower 100s. A developing negative height anomaly near \n40N/50W may cause the eastern portion of the ridge break \ndown/retrograde later next week. However, Thursday likely \nremains quite hot as well. Mainly dry with PoPs near to below \nclimo Monday through Wednesday. Chances of showers/tstms may not\nreturn until Thursday once NW mid-level flow increases as the \neastern flank of the ridge breaks down.",
